            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to Geography</strong><br>\nEratosthenes's contributions to geography are equally significant. He created one of the earliest maps of the world, incorporating the information from explorers and other sources of his time. He coined the terms \"geography,\" \"latitude,\" and \"longitude,\" and was one of the first to suggest that a person could reach India by sailing west from Spain.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Eratosthenes's Work in Astronomy</strong><br>\nIn astronomy, Eratosthenes calculated the tilt of the Earth's axis, which affects the seasons. He also tried to calculate the distance from the Earth to the Sun and the Moon. Although his methods were primitive by today's standards, his work laid the foundation for the development of astronomy as a science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Eratosthenes as a Librarian</strong><br>\nBesides his scientific achievements, Eratosthenes also became the chief librarian at the Library of Alexandria, one of the most significant libraries of the ancient world. His role there not only allowed him to contribute to the preservation and accumulation of knowledge but also to engage with other scholars and intellectuals of his time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
