          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Eratosthenes's Literary Work</strong><br>\nEratosthenes was also an author, writing on topics like philosophy and literary criticism. His work \"Platonicus,\" which discusses the mathematical foundation of Plato's philosophies, reflects his diverse interests and his ability to intersect different fields of thought, though most of his literary works have been lost to time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Final Days of Eratosthenes</strong><br>\nEratosthenes's final days were marred by tragedy, as he became blind in old age. His blindness led him to a deep depression, and it is said that he starved himself to death. He died around 194 BCE, leaving behind a legacy of being a versatile scholar and the most accurate geographer of his time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Eratosthenes</strong><br>\nThe legacy of Eratosthenes lasts to this day, with his methodologies and discoveries still taught in modern classrooms. His work influences the way geography is taught, the understanding of prime numbers in mathematics, and fundamental knowledge in astronomy. His name has been honored by the naming of a crater on the Moon as well as the asteroid 4359 Eratosthenes.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
